# Roguelike Design Patterns for BrogueMCP

This document outlines common design patterns and best practices for roguelike game development in BrogueMCP.

## Procedural Generation

### Dungeon Generation

When implementing procedural dungeon generation:

1. Separate the generation algorithm from the rendering code
2. Implement a modular approach with composable generation steps
3. Use a seed-based approach for reproducible results

Example structure:

```c
// Generation context
typedef struct DungeonGenContext {
    int width;
    int height;
    unsigned int seed;
    // Additional parameters
} DungeonGenContext;

// Generation phases
void initializeDungeon(DungeonGenContext* context, Cell** map);
void placeRooms(DungeonGenContext* context, Cell** map);
void createCorridors(DungeonGenContext* context, Cell** map);
void addFeatures(DungeonGenContext* context, Cell** map);
void populateEntities(DungeonGenContext* context, Cell** map);

// Main generation function
Cell** generateDungeon(int width, int height, unsigned int seed) {
    // Create the map
    Cell** map = allocateMap(width, height);
    
    // Setup context
    DungeonGenContext context;
    context.width = width;
    context.height = height;
    context.seed = seed;
    
    // Execute generation phases
    initializeDungeon(&context, map);
    placeRooms(&context, map);
    createCorridors(&context, map);
    addFeatures(&context, map);
    populateEntities(&context, map);
    
    return map;
}
```

### Random Number Generation

Use a consistent RNG approach throughout the codebase:

```c
// Initialize RNG with seed
void initRNG(unsigned int seed) {
    srand(seed);
}

// Get random integer in range [min, max]
int randomInt(int min, int max) {
    return min + rand() % (max - min + 1);
}

// Get random float in range [0, 1]
float randomFloat() {
    return (float)rand() / RAND_MAX;
}

// Get random boolean with specified probability
bool randomChance(float probability) {
    return randomFloat() < probability;
}
```

## Turn-Based Systems

Implement clean separation between game state and actions:

```c
// Action representation
typedef struct Action {
    int type;
    Entity* actor;
    Entity* target;
    int x, y;  // Target coordinates
    // Additional parameters
} Action;

// Result of an action
typedef struct ActionResult {
    bool success;
    char* message;
    // Effects of the action
} ActionResult;

// Process an action and update game state
ActionResult* processAction(GameState* state, Action* action);

// Manage the game loop
void gameTurn(GameState* state) {
    // Get player action
    Action* playerAction = getPlayerAction(state);
    
    // Process player action
    ActionResult* result = processAction(state, playerAction);
    
    // Update UI with result
    displayActionResult(result);
    
    // Process AI turns
    for (int i = 0; i < state->entityCount; i++) {
        if (state->entities[i]->isAI && state->entities[i]->isActive) {
            Action* aiAction = calculateAIAction(state, state->entities[i]);
            processAction(state, aiAction);
            free(aiAction);
        }
    }
    
    // Cleanup
    free(playerAction);
    free(result);
}
```

## Entity Component System

Consider using an Entity Component System for flexible game objects:

```c
// Component types
typedef enum {
    COMPONENT_POSITION,
    COMPONENT_RENDERABLE,
    COMPONENT_PHYSICS,
    COMPONENT_AI,
    COMPONENT_ITEM,
    // Other component types
    COMPONENT_COUNT
} ComponentType;

// Component base
typedef struct Component {
    ComponentType type;
} Component;

// Position component
typedef struct PositionComponent {
    Component base;
    int x, y;
} PositionComponent;

// Entity
typedef struct Entity {
    int id;
    Component* components[COMPONENT_COUNT];
} Entity;

// Component management
void attachComponent(Entity* entity, Component* component);
Component* getComponent(Entity* entity, ComponentType type);
void removeComponent(Entity* entity, ComponentType type);
```

## Field of View and Lighting

Implement efficient field of view calculations:

```c
// Calculate field of view from position
void calculateFOV(GameState* state, int x, int y, int radius) {
    // Reset visibility
    for (int cy = 0; cy < state->mapHeight; cy++) {
        for (int cx = 0; cx < state->mapWidth; cx++) {
            state->map[cy][cx].visible = false;
        }
    }
    
    // Implement shadowcasting or similar algorithm
    for (int octant = 0; octant < 8; octant++) {
        castLight(state, x, y, radius, 1, 1.0, 0.0, octant);
    }
}

// Update explored state based on visibility
void updateExplored(GameState* state) {
    for (int y = 0; y < state->mapHeight; y++) {
        for (int x = 0; x < state->mapWidth; x++) {
            if (state->map[y][x].visible) {
                state->map[y][x].explored = true;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

